@@ -69,6 +69,35 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Rich can pretty print a JSON document supplied as a string, with syntax highlighting. Use the :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method, which re-serializes the JSON as multi-line output indented by four spaces by default. The output is valid JSON. Here's an example::
+
+    from rich.console import Console
+    console = Console()
+    console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+You can change the indentation with the ``indent`` argument, and disable highlighting with ``highlight=False``::
+
+    console.print_json(data, indent=2, highlight=False)
+
+The JSON is parsed with the standard library, so invalid JSON raises the parser's error and nothing is printed.
+
+The JSON output is also available as a renderable, :class:`~rich.json.JSON`, which you can pass to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`. Output from the renderable is not wrapped, so long lines are kept intact::
+
+    from rich.json import JSON
+    console.log(JSON(data))
+
+The :func:`rich.print_json` function is a shortcut which uses the global console::
+
+    from rich import print_json
+    print_json(data)
+
+Rich also provides a command line tool which pretty prints a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
